#:seon.task{:name "two-plan-passes-start-one-attempt"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card R1 (r1-db-flow-design.md, hybrid-start-a-flow.md H6/H7). `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow` (the one Flow proc in the cluster graph linking Datahike and Flow) reads `seon.cluster.agent/plan-facts` on main (`seon.task/frontier`, open attempts with their task's intent, ended counts, need end commits) and the roster, asks the pure `seon.cluster.agent/plan` for actions and applies each: `:attempt` writes the attempt and its agent in ONE main transaction (`seon.task/attempt-tx` + `seon.cluster.agent/creation-tx`) before anything runs, `:start-flow` calls `seon.cluster.agent/start-flow!` (fork from the fork point, world, environment, graph paused, resumed only under :running). A second pass writes nothing and starts nothing; `:seon.config.agent/max-running` (default 4) counts open attempts. Nothing else starts a graph: `seon.cluster.host/host!` and the per-branch db<->flow graph are deleted. Proofs: seon.cluster.db-flow-test/plan-writes-the-attempt-first-and-makes-flow-follow (the table), the two-step chain test."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow seon.cluster.agent/plan seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.flow/start-graph! seon.cluster.registry/fork! seon.task/frontier seon.task/attempt-tx seon.context/current-state seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/r1-db-flow-design.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "each-intent-names-one-roster-state"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card R2. Intents to Flow verbs: :paused launches without resume or pauses a running graph; :running resumes or re-hosts; :stopped pauses, joins, stops and releases the SCI context keeping agent, branch and attempt; :cancelled interrupts, ends :cancelled with a reason and deletes the branch; cluster :paused wins; `seon.flow/start-graph!` resumes only under the intent. A table over the five intents."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow! seon.flow/start-graph!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "disarm-splits-into-stop-and-cancel"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card D3 (with R2). design-probes P3: `clojure.core.async.flow/stop` then `seon.flow/join-graph!` without `interrupt-turn!` exits after the current transform (Flow reads control with priority between transforms; a transform is never preempted), and `flow/pause` then `flow/ping-proc` under the same bound acknowledges the pass's end. So `seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow!` splits: a :stopped intent is `stop-graph!` minus the interrupt-turn! line, a :cancelled intent keeps it; the bound `:seon.config.agent/turn-completion-backstop-ms` covers a whole pass (its dial already adds the provider or evaluation limit); a pass past the bound gets today's typed join timeout with the entry kept. Proof: R2's table row for :stopped (seon.cluster.agent-test/each-intent-names-one-roster-state)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/stop-flow! seon.flow/join-graph!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/design-probes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase E3 of hybrid-immutable-env.md (H5 option B, decided). A fresh agent's environment is ONE `sci.core/fork` of the frozen program context (`seon.sci.eval/program-context`, phase E2) plus custody, print session, fresh kernel atoms and result objects: `seon.sci.eval/fork-for-agent` (value in, context out) replaces `seon.sci.eval/copy-base-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx`, `seon.sci.eval/branch-ctx` and `seon.sci.eval/fork-for-turn`'s fresh path at `seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context!`, `seon.test`'s request world and `seon.cluster.boot`; the agent keeps the value it forked from; `:my.program/base-ctx` and `:seon.sci.eval/base-ctx` leave the handle. Lazy installs (`seon.sci.kernel/ensure-function!`) land in the agent's own fork; the proof records how many functions the loaded value leaves lazy, deciding whether the derivation installs them eagerly. Proof: seon.cluster.agent-test/a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-two-forks renamed a-fresh-agents-environment-costs-one-fork and tightened: `seon.sci.eval/inherit-var-bindings!` called once, `seon.sci.eval/acquire!` and `seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx` zero, no history reads for an agent that never ran, custody is the branch connection, environment leg under 1 ms and 1 MB beyond the lookup (14.5 ms and 9.1 MB with two forks, 25.6 ms and 16.6 MB with four). Files: src/seon/sci/eval.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (held by lane p4-branch-writes: a patch for root), src/seon/test.clj, src/seon/cluster/boot.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.sci.eval/base-ctx seon.sci.eval/branch-ctx seon.sci.eval/fork-for-turn seon.sci.eval/fork-cluster-ctx seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context! seon.sci.kernel/ensure-function! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-immutable-env.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-paused-intent-starts-a-pingable-graph-that-runs-nothing"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase H6 of hybrid-start-a-flow.md, the :paused row of card R2 (task seon.cluster.agent/each-intent-names-one-roster-state). `seon.flow/start-graph!` takes the task's `:seon.task/execution` and calls `clojure.core.async.flow/resume` only for :running; Flow starts paused. Proof: this test: a graph started under :paused answers :paused from `clojure.core.async.flow/ping` for every proc, a wake offered to its mailbox runs no turn, `clojure.core.async.flow/resume` then runs it; graph start under 15 ms and 5 MB with three procs through `seon.profile/measure` (10 ms once card D2 deletes the schedule proc). Files: src/seon/flow.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.flow/start-graph! seon.cluster.agent/graph-definition seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-start-a-flow.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-received-message-is-written-on-the-recipients-branch-and-rendered-in-its-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase P7 of hybrid-agent-world.md (Opus lane; beside P4 and P5). Messages are channels: the agent graph's mailbox proc (`seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step`, today one payload-free wake in-port on a sliding-buffer 1) gains a second in-port :seon.agent/inbox, a bounded buffer carrying the message value (new dial :seon.config.agent/inbox-size, default 64, resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn), registered in the roster beside the wake channel so `seon.cluster.agent/channels` answers it for `seon.cluster.message/send!`'s offer!. On an inbox message the :io transform writes the message on the agent's OWN connection with `seon.cluster.message/send-call` (identity :seon.message/id, so a repeat is a no-op) and then emits its :seon.agent/episode signal, so the next turn's `seon.context/current-state` renders the inbox from the agent's own branch as today. Nothing is written in transit, nothing relays through main, no other writer touches the branch; a crash between offer and write loses the message (channels are ephemeral), and the sender resends by the same id. Root's graph has the same mailbox, so messages to root are written on main by root's own proc. Proofs: this test (after one offer the recipient's branch holds the message by identity, main and the sender's branch do not, the episode fired once and current-state shows the message; offer -> write under 30 ms warm through seon.profile/measure); a-resent-message-with-the-same-id-writes-nothing (two offers, one datom set, two episodes); a-message-to-root-takes-the-same-path (root's mailbox writes it on main). Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj (mailbox-step, graph-definition, routing), resources/seon/schemas/seon.config.agent.edn, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/mailbox-step seon.cluster.agent/graph-definition seon.cluster.agent/channels seon.cluster.message/send-call seon.context/current-state seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-successor-starts-from-its-predecessors-program-context"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase E6 of hybrid-immutable-env.md (owner decision: a chain step forks its predecessor's end commit, reusing its SCI and db points; supersedes hybrid-agent-world.md's fork-from-main row and closes astra-overnight-review.md P1). `seon.cluster.agent/fork-attempt!` forks the predecessor's end commit persisted on the attempt (`:seon.task.attempt/fork-point`, H8), creates the successor's branch before `seon.task/delete-branch!` removes the predecessor's so Datahike retention owns the history, and the successor's program context is that commit's `seon.sci.eval/program-context` lookup: the predecessor's own value when its final recording changed no program row. The successor's private layer is fresh; a live value moves only by an explicit transfer. No missing-branch fallback to main and no row import. Proof: seon.cluster.agent-test/a-successor-starts-from-its-predecessors-program-context: the predecessor changes a function and ends, main advances differently, the successor executes the predecessor's definition and reads its end facts, none of the predecessor's private Vars is present, `seon.sci.eval/derive-base-ctx` and `seon.sci.eval/interpret-overlay!` are called zero times, environment leg under 2 ms through `seon.profile/measure`; repeated after emptying the kind on a disposable store. After H7, H8, phase E5 and lane p4-branch-writes' release. Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, src/seon/task.clj, test/seon/cluster/agent_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/fork-attempt! seon.cluster.agent/start-flow! seon.task/delete-branch! seon.sci.eval/base-ctx seon.cluster.registry/fork! seon.profile/measure}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-immutable-env.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/hybrid-agent-world.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/astra-overnight-review.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-running-agents-refinement-reaches-main-at-the-next-pass"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Phase U2 of hybrid-push.md (after U1; src/seon/cluster/agent.clj is held by lane p4-branch-writes: wait for its release or hand it the slice). `seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow` is the one auto-merger (owner: agents never push; an attempt's facts reach main only through its merge request, opened with the attempt, one per attempt, `seon.task/request-merge!`'s recurrence key becoming the attempt; its contents are the derived diff since :seon.task.attempt/merged): `seon.cluster.wake/route!` offers it a wake on every branch transaction that asserts or retracts a :seon.cluster attribute (derived from `seon.program/cluster-attributes`, never a list); its pass derives `seon.cluster.agent/unmerged` (renamed from `unlanded-ends`, meaning changed: the attempts whose branch head commit differs from :seon.task.attempt/merged, read on main) and for each, in sorted branch order, transacts on main [[:db.fn/call seon.cluster.squash/auto-merge-tx attempt head]] with :tx-meta provenance (source branch, source commit); merged = head transacts nothing. The agent's world never names main; a crash before the auto-merge is re-derived by the ::flow/resume prime. Proofs: this test (main holds the refined :seon.task/intent while the graph still runs and :seon.task.attempt/merged equals the branch head and the merge request exists); an-evaluation-without-tracked-facts-writes-nothing-on-main (main's basis unchanged after an evaluation-only interval); an-unmerged-branch-after-a-crash-is-merged-once-by-the-prime (a branch write with no pass, then resume: exactly one main transaction); cost rows through `seon.profile/measure`: recording to main visibility under 100 ms; p99 writer latency with ten branches recorded. Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, src/seon/cluster/wake.clj, src/seon/task.clj (request-merge!), test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/db<->flow seon.cluster.agent/unlanded-ends seon.cluster.wake/route! seon.cluster.wake/starting-attributes seon.program/cluster-attributes seon.task/request-merge! seon.db/transact!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-push.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-successor-whose-proof-its-predecessor-wrote-starts"
  :kind :function
  :intent "Phase P5 of hybrid-step-semantics.md (F3). A chain step whose proof its predecessor wrote cannot start: `seon.cluster.agent/plan-facts` judges a frontier leaf valid by `seon.task/task-edges` on main, but the predecessor's proof lives on its attempt branch, so the leaf is :invalid, `seon.cluster.agent/plan` writes the problem task seon.task/invalid-task-<handle> and never attempts it. Observed on default 2319152dc (chain experiment 1): trailing-prose-implement became ready when Design+Prove ended :done and was refused for want of a proof that its fork point held. Correct: validity reads `seon.task/obligations` on the value the leaf will fork from (the done needs' end commits in `:seon.flow.plan/basis` opened as a database, else main), cached by commit id, the same value its first pass proves on. An :implement step needs its own proofs at that value; a :prove step needs its own proofs or a successor declaring it in :seon.task/needs (the product is what it will write, not an admission input); `seon.cluster.agent/write-problem!`'s hardcoded 'names no proof' intent becomes the failed check as data. Proof: seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-successor-whose-proof-its-predecessor-wrote-starts (chain-experiment-1-proofs.patch). Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj."
  :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/plan-facts seon.cluster.agent/plan seon.task/obligations datahike.api/commit-as-db seon.cluster.agent/write-problem!}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/step-p5-p6-landing.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-step-semantics.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-failed-start-ends-its-attempt-and-the-next-plans"
  :kind :function
  :intent "Phase P7 of hybrid-step-semantics.md (verify-astra #6). `seon.cluster.agent/apply-plan!` catches each action's failure and goes on, so a failed :attempt write still runs the dependent :start-flow against a missing row, and the next pass replans the same first action: a fence over head-of-line blocking. Correct: :attempt and :start-flow are one unit in `seon.cluster.agent/write-attempt!` + `seon.cluster.agent/start-attempt!`; a failure writes that attempt's `:seon.task.attempt/end` :error with its reason (the entrant boundary's translation), so plan stops issuing it and root gets its repair problem (P9); the per-action catch and db<->flow's start catch go. Proof: seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-failed-start-ends-its-attempt-and-the-next-plans (a refused acquisition ends attempt 1 :error in the same pass; the next pass starts the other ready leaf and never retries attempt 1). Files: src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj."
  :must-read #{seon.cluster.agent/apply-plan! seon.cluster.agent/write-attempt! seon.cluster.agent/start-attempt! seon.cluster.agent/plan}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-step-semantics.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-capped-attempt-has-no-relaunch"
  :kind :function
  :intent "Phase P9 of hybrid-step-semantics.md (F5). A step whose attempt ended :capped, :stalled, :given-back or :error leaves the chain stopped with no way on: `seon.task/frontier` excludes the leaf for good, root receives only an end message (no repair problem task, runtime-follows-db §5), and nothing starts attempt 2. Chain experiment 1 relaunched twice by hand (a new step task with the needs rewired; an end rewritten from :capped to :sent-back). Correct: the end is the attempt's own fact; `seon.cluster.agent/plan` writes one repair problem task by identity [class attempt] assigned to root, carrying the end and reason; root's `my.root/relaunch!` writes `:seon.task.attempt/relaunch` (its reason) on the ended attempt, with optional `:seon.task.attempt/relaunch-fork` (a commit root names instead), and the ready rule reads 'an ended attempt of a terminal class with no relaunch' (one not), so attempt n+1 starts from the ended attempt's fork point (a plain restart) or the named commit; root may adjust the environment before it starts; no end is rewritten and nothing retries by itself. The repair problem's proof: its attempt carries a relaunch or its task is :held or :cancelled. Proof: seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-capped-attempt-relaunches-only-by-roots-fact (capped -> problem, no attempt 2; relaunch -> attempt 2 forked from attempt 1's fork point; a relaunch naming a commit forks that commit); rows of seon.task/each-problem-class-is-detected-from-its-facts. Files: src/seon/task.clj, src/seon/cluster/agent.clj, src/my/root.clj, test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj."
  :must-read #{seon.task/frontier seon.cluster.agent/plan seon.turn/end-task-call seon.task/create! my.root/merge!}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/hybrid-step-semantics.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-12/chain-experiment-1.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-real-chain-runs-with-no-nudges"
  :kind :function
  :intent "P-final of hybrid-step-semantics.md. After P1-P10 land and default restarts on that HEAD, the chain test seon.cluster.db-flow-test/a-green-first-step-ends-itself-and-its-end-starts-the-next-from-its-commit is extended to Design+Prove (an expected red product) -> Implement -> one root merge request, with no hand end edits, no copying of proofs to main and no successor launched by hand (injected provider replies); then chain experiment 1's subject (`seon.sci.forgive/segments`, trailing prose) reruns paid with the same instructions plus Prove's rule 'run the tests reaching the subject against the designed shape': expected about 5 + 4 calls, at most one send-back, a merge request in root's queue, zero nudges. Files: test/seon/cluster/db_flow_test.clj; the run's note under docs/research/agent-platform/."
